From 67169c68fa325969853313a1628dc0fbc43206bc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: telegdyd <telegdyd93@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:25:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] jasmine-j0031: SweetGPU buffers are cacheable by default
 (sweetgpu_wc=1 for write-combined)

j0029's run with sweetgpu_wc=0 got Mesa's screen up with no freeze, where
every write-combined run froze in the first buffer creation. Cacheable
buffers with clean/invalidate around GPU work are now the default;
sweetgpu_wc=1 brings the write-combined buffers back (to catch that freeze
with j0030's panic over USB).
---
 iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetGPU.cpp | 15 ++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetGPU.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetGPU.cpp
index 9f90639..299cedc 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetGPU.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetGPU.cpp
@@ -24,12 +24,13 @@
  * 1 s and, if the job did not finish, logs the fault state and takes the GPU
  * down (sweet_gpu_hang_check) instead of letting a stuck CP hold the bus.
  *
- * Boot-arg sweetgpu_wc=0 makes buffers plain cacheable memory, in the
- * kernel and in the client's mapping, instead of write-combined: the kernel
- * then cleans and invalidates all of the client's buffers around each
- * submit, wait and completion query (the GPU's SMMU walk and accesses are
- * not coherent with the CPU caches). A write-combined IOBufferMemoryDescriptor
- * changes the cache attributes of its physical pages for every mapping.
+ * Buffers are plain cacheable memory, in the kernel and in the client's
+ * mapping: the kernel cleans and invalidates all of the client's buffers
+ * around each submit, wait and completion query (the GPU's SMMU walk and
+ * accesses are not coherent with the CPU caches). Write-combined buffers
+ * (boot-arg sweetgpu_wc=1, a write-combined IOBufferMemoryDescriptor, which
+ * changes the cache attributes of its physical pages for every mapping)
+ * froze the phone in their first creation (2026-10-21, j0028/j0029).
  *
  * Buffers are pageable-free kernel memory (IOBufferMemoryDescriptor), mapped
  * into the one GPU address space with 4K pages; a client's buffers are
@@ -61,7 +62,7 @@ void sweet_gpu_publish(void);
 }
 
 static uint32_t sweet_gpu_debug;
-static uint32_t sweet_gpu_wc = 1;
+static uint32_t sweet_gpu_wc;
 
 /* debug log line, then a pause so the USB log carries it out before the GPU runs anything */
 #define GPULOG(fmt, ...) do {                                           \
-- 
